SAVED QUEUE BACKUP RECOVERY — BOUNDED SYNTHETIC QUALIFICATION
=========================================================

Run scripts/check_queue_backup_recovery.py with retained trusted --backend-image,
--valkey-image, optional --docker, and a NEW absolute private POSIX --work-dir.
The runner resolves immutable image IDs. It does not accept installation source
containers, volumes or customer backups and is NOT a production migration tool.
Only its report.json is shareable. Other files contain synthetic credentials,
task arguments, envelopes and private backup data and must not be uploaded.

The fixture creates 120 legacy Celery jobs across DB0/1/2, five queues and four
priority buckets, reserves one delivery, saves an RDB, then creates a multipart
AOF and adds a newer task after its base rewrite. After the owned server stops,
the entire RDB/AOF set and file hashes are retained privately. All loads use
new network=none containers, read-only roots, bounded writable tmpfs and no
published ports, production volumes or hardware access. Images are not deleted.

Acceptance checks:
- RDB-only and AOF-only copies load; a mixed set uses the newer AOF state.
- Mandatory logical-data comparison detects a healthy-but-empty wrong-mode load
  and a healthy-but-stale RDB load. Server readiness alone is not proof.
- Lists retain order; sets/hashes are canonicalized independently of their
  internal serialization. All 16 fixture databases, types, values and absolute
  expiry timestamps are compared. Only elapsed expected TTLs may disappear.
- Missing multipart segments/manifests and unexpected members are refused.
  Link, traversal, duplicate and malformed archive/manifest cases fail closed.
- aof-load-truncated=no refuses partial incremental tails. RDB checksum and
  malformed AOF failures stay disconnected. No check-aof --fix is performed.
- After loading, persistence is disabled on the disposable copy before the
  separately qualified read-only queue inventory runs.
- An explicit SYNTHETIC-only review approves 120 queued messages, chooses the
  cross-DB merge order, and quarantines the reserved delivery. A real Celery
  worker consumes these on a separate empty target. Only an inert fixture task
  is registered; no FreeSDN hardware modules are imported. Per-queue FIFO,
  unique execution counts, results and empty unacked state are verified.
- Original stopped-source files, the retained backup archive, and the loaded
  review copy are checked again after consumption. Abandoning a target before
  its first write preserves the retained backup.

Limits which remain release gates:
- This exact-version synthetic fixture does not select the authoritative old
  primary, qualify older Redis formats, or establish customer backup completeness.
  Preserve both HA data volumes and Sentinel identity/epoch state. Source role,
  election history, replication offsets and fencing need site evidence.
- A multipart AOF backup needs its complete manifest, base and incremental files.
  A running server can rewrite/remove segments during a copy. The rehearsal
  fences its own source before copying; it does not provide live backup capture.
- A file hash proves the copied bytes, not that all original work was captured.
  Capture expected source counts and semantic fingerprints BEFORE restore; never
  use the restored target's own inventory as the only expected inventory.
- Empty, expired or missing result records never authorize task replay. Reserved
  deliveries and duplicate/conflicting jobs require application/device review.
  The synthetic cross-DB order is an explicit test decision, not an inferred
  historical global ordering or a customer migration policy.
- No production queue replay, HA volume adoption, password rotation, coordinated
  PostgreSQL/queue cutover, customer backup acceptance, host-loss recovery,
  production-scale RTO/RPO or rollback after target/device writes is qualified.
- Cleanup is limited to this run's exact owned container IDs and labels. On
  engine failure, report.json remains incomplete and cleanup-required.json names
  pending owned resources. Inspect that private record before manual cleanup.

Read QUEUE-INVENTORY and HA-UPGRADING before planning an existing-site migration.
